    text: "English has an extraordinarily rich set of movement verbs, each conveying not just motion but manner, speed, and attitude. Walking alone branches into dozens of variations: you stroll through a park (leisurely), stride across a room (purposefully), trudge through mud (wearily), shuffle along a corridor (slowly, dragging feet), tiptoe past a sleeping child (quietly), and stomp up the stairs (angrily). Running has similar range: you sprint to catch a bus (fast, short burst), jog around the neighbourhood (steady exercise pace), dash to the shops (hurry briefly), and bolt from danger (flee suddenly). Climbing vocabulary includes scramble (climb awkwardly using hands and feet), clamber (climb with difficulty), and scale (climb something very high or steep). Jumping expands into leap (jump far or high with energy), hop (jump on one foot or small jumps), skip (step-hop alternating feet, playful), and bound (jump with large, energetic strides). Water movement gives us wade (walk through water), paddle (shallow playful water movement), and plunge (dive or fall suddenly into water). Each verb paints a different picture, and choosing the right one transforms flat prose into vivid narrative. A character doesn't just 'walk' — they saunter, limp, march, or creep, and each word tells you something about their mood, physical state, and intention.",
